    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess before initiating pulmonary rehabilitation:\n- **COPD severity**: GOLD classification (based on post-bronchodilator FEV1), COPD Assessment Test (CAT) score, exacerbation history (2+ moderate or 1+ severe exacerbation in past year = high risk).\n- **Symptom burden**: MRC dyspnea scale, Borg dyspnea scale with activity, dyspnea on ADLs (bathing, dressing, eating).\n- **Exercise limitation**: Deconditioning vs. ventilatory limitation vs. cardiac limitation vs. musculoskeletal limitation. Often multifactorial in COPD.\n- **Comorbidities**: Screen for cardiovascular disease (CAD, heart failure, pulmonary hypertension), osteoporosis (steroid use, smoking), anxiety and depression (40% prevalence in COPD), skeletal muscle wasting/sarcopenia, malnutrition (BMI, albumin).\n- **Nutritional status**: Unintentional weight loss is a poor prognostic indicator in COPD. Calculate BMI and assess dietary intake. Cachexia is common in severe COPD.\n- **Smoking status**: Current use, readiness to quit, prior quit attempts, nicotine replacement therapy history. Active smoking is NOT a contraindication to pulmonary rehab but cessation counseling must be integrated.\n- **Oxygen use**: Compliance, prescribed flow rate, documented desaturation on prior testing. Address barriers to use.\n- **Vaccination status**: Influenza (annual), pneumococcal (PCV15 or PCV20, PPSV23), COVID-19, Tdap.\n- **Advance care planning**: Has the patient considered goals of care? COPD has a progressive, terminal trajectory — discuss early while functional.',
