    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss the evaluation of exercise capacity in COPD, contraindications to exercise in pulmonary rehabilitation, and the criteria for supplemental oxygen prescription.',
      content: '**Exercise Capacity Evaluation**:\n\n1. **6-Minute Walk Test (6MWT)**: The most commonly used and practical test for pulmonary rehab. Measures submaximal exercise capacity. Normal distance is approximately 400-700 meters depending on age, sex, and height. A **minimum clinically important difference (MCID) is 30 meters** for COPD.\n\n2. **Incremental Shuttle Walk Test (ISWT)**: Externally paced, progressive test. Patient walks between two cones 10 meters apart at increasing speeds signaled by audio beeps. More reproducible than 6MWT and better correlates with VO2 max. MCID is 47.5 meters.\n\n3. **Cardiopulmonary Exercise Testing (CPET)**: Gold standard for differentiating ventilatory vs. cardiac vs. musculoskeletal limitation. Measures VO2 peak, anaerobic threshold, VE/VCO2 slope, heart rate response, oxygen pulse. Indicated when the cause of exercise limitation is unclear or when cardiac disease is suspected.\n\n**This patient\'s limitations are likely multifactorial**: severe ventilatory limitation (FEV1 38%), deconditioning (skeletal muscle wasting), exercise-induced hypoxemia (desaturation to 84%), and anxiety/fear-avoidance behavior.\n\n**Contraindications to Exercise in Pulmonary Rehab**:\n- **Absolute**: Unstable angina, recent MI (within 4 weeks), uncontrolled heart failure, active pulmonary embolism, resting SpO2 < 85% on supplemental O2, uncontrolled arrhythmia, severe pulmonary hypertension (mPAP > 55 mmHg), aortic dissection.\n- **Relative**: Orthopedic limitations that prevent exercise, uncontrolled hypertension (SBP > 200), significant cognitive impairment that limits participation.\n- **Active smoking is NOT a contraindication** — smokers benefit from pulmonary rehab and smoking cessation should be incorporated.\n\n**Supplemental Oxygen Criteria (CMS/Medicare)**:\n- **Continuous O2 (Group I)**: PaO2 <= 55 mmHg OR SpO2 <= 88% at rest on room air.\n- **Continuous O2 (Group II)**: PaO2 56-59 mmHg OR SpO2 89% WITH evidence of end-organ hypoxia (polycythemia with Hct > 56%, pulmonary hypertension on echo, cor pulmonale with peripheral edema).\n- **Ambulatory/Exercise O2**: Demonstrated desaturation to SpO2 <= 88% during ambulation or exercise with documented improvement with O2.\n\n**This patient qualifies for supplemental O2**: SpO2 91% at rest (borderline) but desaturates to 84% during exercise. He needs **3 L/min O2 with activity** (titrated to maintain SpO2 >= 88-90% during the 6MWT). He should also have a repeat resting ABG to determine if he meets criteria for continuous O2.\n\n**Oxygen titration during rehab**: Adjust flow rate to maintain SpO2 >= 88-90% during exercise. Use pulse oximetry continuously during exercise sessions. Reassess oxygen needs every 60-90 days and after exacerbations.',
