    const Section(
      title: '+ Design a comprehensive pulmonary rehabilitation program for this patient including exercise prescription, education, and self-management components.',
      content: '**Pulmonary Rehabilitation Program Structure**:\n- **Duration**: 12-16 weeks (minimum 24 sessions for Medicare coverage), typically 2-3 sessions per week.\n- **Session length**: 60-90 minutes per session.\n- **Setting**: Outpatient hospital-based or freestanding pulmonary rehab center.\n- **Team**: Pulmonologist (medical director), physiatrist or rehab physician, respiratory therapist, exercise physiologist, physical therapist, occupational therapist, dietitian, psychologist/social worker.\n\n**Exercise Prescription**:\n\n1. **Endurance (Aerobic) Training** (core component):\n- **Modalities**: Treadmill walking, stationary cycling (upright or recumbent), NuStep (combined upper and lower body).\n- **Intensity**: Start at 60-80% of peak work rate from ISWT or baseline 6MWT. Use modified Borg dyspnea scale (target 4-6 out of 10) for intensity guidance.\n- **Duration**: Start at 10-15 minutes continuous (or interval training if unable to sustain continuous), progress to 30-45 minutes.\n- **Interval training**: For patients unable to sustain continuous exercise. Alternate 1-2 minutes of higher intensity with 1-2 minutes of rest. Equally effective as continuous training in COPD.\n- **Supplemental O2**: 3 L/min via nasal cannula during exercise, titrate to maintain SpO2 >= 88%.\n\n2. **Resistance (Strength) Training**:\n- Address peripheral muscle weakness (quadriceps, deltoids, biceps) which is an independent predictor of mortality in COPD.\n- Start at 40-60% of 1-RM for upper and lower body. 2-3 sets of 8-12 repetitions. Progress by 5-10% when the patient can complete 3 sets of 12 comfortably.\n- Key muscle groups: quadriceps (leg press, knee extension), hamstrings, hip abductors, biceps, deltoids, latissimus dorsi.\n\n3. **Breathing Retraining**:\n- **Pursed-lip breathing (PLB)**: Inhale through nose for 2 counts, exhale through pursed lips for 4-6 counts. Reduces respiratory rate, increases tidal volume, prevents dynamic hyperinflation, and reduces dyspnea.\n- **Diaphragmatic breathing**: Place hand on abdomen, focus on diaphragmatic excursion. Limited evidence but may help some patients.\n- **Active expiratory technique**: During exercise, coordinate breathing with exertion — exhale during the effort phase.\n\n4. **Flexibility and Balance**:\n- Chest wall stretching (pectoralis, intercostal stretching) to improve thoracic mobility.\n- Balance training to reduce fall risk (COPD patients have 2-3x fall risk vs. age-matched controls due to deconditioning, medications, and hypoxia).\n\n**Education and Self-Management**:\n5. **Disease education**: COPD pathophysiology, medication technique (inhaler review — up to 50% of patients use inhalers incorrectly), oxygen therapy rationale.\n6. **Action plan for exacerbations**: Written plan for recognizing early symptoms (increased sputum, color change, increased dyspnea) and initiating antibiotics + corticosteroids per standing prescription.\n7. **Energy conservation and pacing**: OT-led sessions on ADL modification, activity pacing, work simplification.\n8. **Nutritional counseling**: Address sarcopenia with protein optimization (1.2-1.5 g/kg/day), caloric supplementation if underweight, small frequent meals (large meals worsen dyspnea by elevating the diaphragm).\n9. **Anxiety and depression management**: CBT, relaxation techniques, mindfulness. Consider pharmacotherapy (SSRI) if significant — avoid benzodiazepines (respiratory depression risk).\n\n**Expected Outcomes of Pulmonary Rehab** (to share with patient):\n- Reduced dyspnea (strongest evidence)\n- Improved exercise capacity (30-50 meter improvement in 6MWD)\n- Improved quality of life (SGRQ, CAT scores)\n- Reduced hospital readmissions and emergency visits\n- Improved depression and anxiety\n- Improved survival when combined with optimal medical therapy\n- **Pulmonary rehab does NOT improve FEV1** — but it dramatically improves the patient\'s experience of living with COPD.',
