      content: '**Medicare Coverage for Pulmonary Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Qualifying diagnoses** (CMS-approved):\n- Moderate to very severe COPD (GOLD II-IV, FEV1 < 80% predicted) — this patient qualifies\n- Asthma (under specific criteria)\n- Interstitial lung disease\n- Other chronic lung diseases causing dyspnea\n\n2. **Coverage specifics**:\n- Medicare Part B covers up to **36 sessions** of pulmonary rehab.\n- An additional **36 sessions** (total 72) may be approved if the physician documents continued medical necessity and functional improvement.\n- Sessions must be supervised by a physician and provided by qualified staff.\n- Requires a physician prescription and documented face-to-face encounter.\n- Patient pays the standard 20% coinsurance (or supplemental insurance covers it).\n- Sessions must include: exercise training, education, psychosocial assessment, outcomes assessment.\n\n3. **Documentation requirements**: Physician must document: diagnosis with supporting PFTs, treatment goals, individualized plan, progress notes after each session or at minimum every 30 days.\n\n4. **Maintenance programs**: After completing formal pulmonary rehab, maintenance exercise is critical but NOT covered by Medicare. Patients must self-fund gym memberships or home equipment. This is a significant barrier.\n\n**Smoking Cessation Strategies**:\n\n1. **Brief Intervention (5 A\'s framework)**:\n- **Ask**: Document smoking status at every visit.\n- **Advise**: \"Quitting smoking is the single most important thing you can do for your lungs. I strongly advise you to quit.\"\n- **Assess**: Readiness to quit (Prochaska stages of change). This patient relapsed — assess triggers and barriers.\n- **Assist**: Provide resources (see below).\n- **Arrange**: Follow-up within 1-2 weeks of quit date.\n\n2. **Pharmacotherapy** (first-line):\n- **Combination nicotine replacement therapy (NRT)**: Long-acting (patch — 21 mg/day for 4 weeks, then taper) PLUS short-acting (nicotine gum 2 mg or lozenge PRN for cravings). Combination NRT is more effective than single-agent.\n- **Varenicline (Chantix)**: Most effective single agent. Partial nicotine receptor agonist. Start 0.5 mg daily x 3 days, then 0.5 mg BID x 4 days, then 1 mg BID for 12 weeks. Start 1-2 weeks before quit date.\n- **Bupropion SR**: 150 mg daily x 3 days, then 150 mg BID for 12 weeks. Especially useful if comorbid depression. Can be combined with NRT.\n\n3. **Behavioral support**:\n- Individual or group counseling (even brief 10-minute counseling increases quit rates).\n- **Quitline**: 1-800-QUIT-NOW (free coaching, NRT samples).\n- **SmokeFreeTXT**: Text-based support program.\n- Pulmonary rehab itself provides a supportive environment for cessation.\n\n4. **Medicare coverage for cessation**: Medicare covers 2 quit attempts per year, each including up to 4 counseling sessions (8 total sessions/year). Pharmacotherapy (varenicline, bupropion, NRT) covered under Part D.\n\n5. **Motivational interviewing approach for relapse**: \"I know quitting is hard — the average person tries 7-10 times before quitting for good. The fact that you quit for 2 years tells me you can do it. That was not a failure; it was practice. What do you think triggered the relapse, and how can we plan for that trigger next time?\"',
