    const Section(
      title: '+ Demonstrate motivational interviewing techniques for smoking cessation in a patient who has relapsed and feels hopeless.',
      content: 'I would use motivational interviewing principles — express empathy, develop discrepancy, roll with resistance, and support self-efficacy:\n\n**1. Express empathy and avoid confrontation**:\n\"I am not here to lecture you or nag you — you get enough of that at home. I can see you are frustrated, and I want you to know that I am not judging you. Quitting smoking is one of the hardest things a person can do, and the fact that you were able to quit for two years tells me something important about you — you have done it before.\"\n\n**2. Roll with resistance (do NOT argue)**:\n\"You said what is the point because your lungs are already damaged. I understand why you might feel that way. Can I share some information with you that might change that perspective, or would you rather not hear it right now?\"\n*Wait for permission — this respects autonomy.*\n\n**3. Develop discrepancy (if he agrees to hear it)**:\n\"Here is something I want you to consider. You are right that we cannot undo the damage that has been done. But continuing to smoke accelerates the decline of your remaining lung function by about 50-60 mL of FEV1 per year, compared to about 30 mL per year if you quit. That difference is the difference between being independent at home for years versus needing more and more oxygen and potentially ending up in the hospital again. Quitting now still makes a meaningful difference for your future.\"\n\n**4. Explore ambivalence**:\n\"It sounds like part of you wants to quit — otherwise you would not have tried or felt guilty about it. What are the things you liked about not smoking during those two years?\"\n*Listen actively. Reflect back what he says.*\n\n**5. Support self-efficacy**:\n\"Two years is a long time to be smoke-free. That is not a failure — that is a success. What we need to figure out is what triggered the relapse and how to prepare you for that trigger next time. Research shows that the average person quits 7-10 times before it sticks permanently. You are not starting over; you are continuing a process.\"\n\n**6. Offer concrete help without pressure**:\n\"When you are ready — and only when you are ready — I can offer you tools that make quitting significantly easier. There are medications like varenicline that reduce cravings and make cigarettes less satisfying. We can also connect you with a free quit coach through the national quitline. But this is your decision. I will support you whether you quit today, next month, or next year. My job is to be honest with you and to help you when you are ready.\"\n\n**7. Address the family dynamic**:\n\"You mentioned your wife nagging you. That usually comes from a place of love and fear — she is scared of losing you. But I understand that pressure can backfire. Would it be helpful if I spoke with her about how to be supportive without adding pressure? Sometimes when we reframe the approach, it takes the tension out of it.\"\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Motivational interviewing (OARS — open questions, affirmations, reflections, summaries), rolling with resistance instead of confronting, respecting patient autonomy, accurate medical information delivered without judgment, practical support resources.',
